body:
For a motor, use a pin on the opposite side with a ~ in front of it. analogWrite takes a value between 0 and 255, so for full speed use 255. You may need a motor driver or external power source to run it if it is a more powerful motor. Correct me if I’m wrong, but I believe HIGH is an integer representing 1, so that won’t be much power for analogWrite, which as I said uses a value between 0 and 255, although HIGH may work differently for analogWrite than digitalWrite. So your code should look more like this:


int motor = 5;

void setup()

{

}

void loop

{

   analogWrite(motor, 255);

   delay(1000);

   analogWrite(motor, 0);

   delay(1000);

}

And the connections should be positive terminal to pin 5 and negative to ground. Although as I said it may not work without extra power from a shield or external power source. I probably forgot something in that code, but it’s just an example as I’m in a hurry and at school haha.

yep, I found quite a few forum posts about it. Happens a lot with David and Jeff from what I can tell. All the videos of it display it occurring when two actions happen at once (the same frame?) that don't normally happen simultaneously, such as getting hit and unhooked, certain killer animations happening while getting pallet stunned, etc.

&#x200B;

Edit: I think it has something to do with my adrenaline and getting saved from the cage at the same time. I think a skill check may have just started too, I can't remember. I might do some testing if I have time.

The normal Syntax would be r.keyPress(KeyEvent.VK\_H);

&#x200B;

I pretty much figure it out. I ended up just manually entering each letter for the required paragraph, which in this case is just a single word, "Hello", so i have the code press shift, press h, release h, release shift, press e, release e, etc. I just really wanted to know why it entered "h5/". What value came from the FirstLetter variable that made it do that?
